questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where order statuses are intermittently not updating in the database, and concurrent modification exceptions occur under load. You inspect the following Spring Service method:

java
@Service
public class OrderFulfillmentService {
    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Autowired
    private InventoryClient inventoryClient;

    @Transactional
    public CompletableFuture<Void> fulfillOrder(Long orderId) {
        Order order = orderRepository.findById(orderId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException("Order not found"));

        return CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            inventoryClient.reserveStock(order.getId());
            order.setStatus(OrderStatus.FULFILLED);
            orderRepository.save(order);
        });
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an API aggregator combines responses from three downstream services via `CompletableFuture`. A severe downstream degradation causes the orchestrator to exceed its overall 500ms SLA, despite setting individual timeouts. You review the aggregation logic:

java
CompletableFuture<User> userFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> userService.getUser(id), customExecutor)
    .orTimeout(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);

CompletableFuture<Order> orderFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> orderService.getOrder(id), customExecutor)
    .orTimeout(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);

CompletableFuture<Credit> creditFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> creditService.getCredit(id), customExecutor)
    .orTimeout(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);

CompletableFuture.allOf(userFuture, orderFuture, creditFuture).join();


When `orderService` hangs indefinitely and triggers its `orTimeout`, the overall call still blocks for significantly longer than 200ms.
